{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next/skills`. It selects exactly the five `SKILL.md` files under `assets/skills`: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `name:` and for leading `---` front matter under `assets` found no other file with skill front matter. The `references` directory under `habeas` has no front matter. The query therefore selects every bundled skill and nothing outside the requirement's scope.\n\n**Query snippets.** The `match` snippet is a document with front matter followed by a heading. The `no_match` snippet is the same heading without front matter. Together they show what `minus_metadata` selects and what it leaves out. That is adequate for a one-condition query.\n\n**Obligation and test.** The requirement says each skill's workflow ends by naming the next-task skill, or one skill per outcome. The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_workflows_end_by_naming_the_skill_for_the_next_task]` takes the last numbered step of each skill's \"Workflow\" section. It does this through `workflow_steps`, which reads the numbered steps and their continuation lines. It then asserts with `assert_text` that the step contains the expected phrases.\n- `habeas-requirements` must name the `habeas-evidence` skill.\n- `habeas-evidence` must name a return to the `habeas-requirements` skill for the outcomes that need code, tests or requirement text changes.\n- `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` must name the `habeas-requirements` skill for gap-list items the user decides.\n- `habeas-refactor` must name both the `habeas-evidence` skill and the `habeas-requirements` skill. These are the two outcomes, so the multi-outcome alternative is tested.\n- `habeas` ends with \"Follow that skill\". The test also checks that the routing table in step 1 contains a `| name |` row for every other skill in `SKILLS`. This is what makes \"that skill\" name the right skills.\n\nThe phrases match the actual last steps in the skill sources. For example, the `habeas-evidence` Workflow step 7 reads \"Next: for findings that need code or tests, or requirement text that must change, return to the habeas-requirements skill.\" All five skills are exercised.\n\n**Gaps.** None. The \"no further task needs no skill\" case imposes no obligation to test. The assertions are phrase-containment checks, so they are not exhaustive. They do pin the named skill for each outcome in each skill. I found no plausible requirement violation that the test would accept. The requirement has no violation queries.",
  "references": "cad77bef8fd9a6e827e81314c255639bcbcc74e3c865da250136997ac253fa61",
